---
// "How to use ULC?" homepage section: one intro paragraph + two audience
// cards (designers, manufacturers). Copy sourced from the upstream README's
// "Who uses ULC" framing.
---

<section class="relative">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 py-20 md:px-10 md:py-28 lg:py-32">
    <h2
      class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight leading-tight max-w-2xl"
    >
      How to use ULC?
    </h2>

    <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-base md:text-lg text-[var(--text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
      ULC is read, not opened. Design tools, spec databases, data pipelines,
      and general-purpose LLMs like ChatGPT and Claude parse a ULC record
      directly, with no PDF-extraction step. Lighting-domain specialty tools go
      further, fetching the linked IES and LDT files on demand and resolving
      industry shorthand. Both work; the difference is depth, not whether ULC is
      consumable.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2 md:gap-10">
      <article
        class="border border-token rounded-lg p-8 md:p-10"
        style="background: var(--bg-elevated);"
      >
        <h3 class="small-caps text-xs text-[var(--text-subtle)]">
          For lighting designers
        </h3>
        <p
          class="mt-3 text-base md:text-lg text-[var(--text)] leading-relaxed"
        >
          Reclaim the hours that disappear into cross-referencing PDFs. ULC
          records read straight into your fixture comparisons, luminaire
          schedules, and VE reviews, every attribute traceable to the
          manufacturer's source. The tools you already use, and any LLM, can
          read ULC today.
        </p>
        <a
          href="/for-designers"
          class="mt-6 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-medium text-[var(--accent-warm)] hover:underline underline-offset-4"
        >
          Designer track <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </article>

      <article
        class="border border-token rounded-lg p-8 md:p-10"
        style="background: var(--bg-elevated);"
      >
        <h3 class="small-caps text-xs text-[var(--text-subtle)]">
          For lighting manufacturers
        </h3>
        <p
          class="mt-3 text-base md:text-lg text-[var(--text)] leading-relaxed"
        >
          Show up when a designer asks an AI which fixture fits. Publish a ULC
          record alongside the PDF, IES, and LDT files you already produce, and
          your products surface in AI-mediated discovery with the attributes you
          published, not whatever an extraction layer guessed. You own the data
          at its source.
        </p>
        <a
          href="/for-manufacturers"
          class="mt-6 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-medium text-[var(--accent-warm)] hover:underline underline-offset-4"
        >
          Manufacturer track <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
